From e3f391a6eafd9fe8afe89d951111e947e004b956 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Hades <15710513+hadesdevls@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Thu, 15 Oct 2026 22:48:41 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] windowing/gbm: look at every display when a video starts, and
 take HDR from what it has

Since 1.0 RC1 the display is looked at again when each video starts (never in the middle of one),
but only once a display that offers Dolby Vision has been seen in the session, or after a
failure of native output, so that nothing changed for a display without it. A TV whose EDID had
no Dolby Vision block when Kodi started, or that had none or one that could not be read (the PC
starts while the TV is off, with Deep Colour off on its input, or behind a receiver in standby),
was never looked at again: no native Dolby Vision, and the HDR of that EDID, until Kodi was
started again.

Now every display is looked at (CDisplayLook). It is probed, as before, where Dolby Vision was
seen or native output failed, and also after a hot-plug event of the device in use since the
last look, which CDRMDisplayWatch now counts: nvidia-drm and i915 have the new EDID only once
the connector is probed. Any other display is looked at by the EDID that the kernel has now
(drmModeGetConnectorCurrent(): nothing is read from the display; a call to the kernel and a
comparison of the bytes, 65 ns besides that call on the host). Another EDID that can be read
replaces the display information of the decisions of Dolby Vision and, from now on, of HDR
(GetDisplayHDRCapabilities(), SetHDR(), the conversion of Dolby Vision to HDR10), and one line
of the log says what the display takes before and after. None, or one that cannot be read,
replaces nothing: HDR10 is never lost to a read at the wrong moment, which is what an earlier version found
when it probed every display. The following of displays ranks the output in use by what its
display takes now (SetInUseDolbyVision()). A hot-plug that the following of displays does not
hear is seen on amdgpu, whose EDID property follows it, and with the other drivers only at the
next one that it hears.

A host model of the kernel, the display and the look runs both ways on amdgpu and on the drivers
that need a probe: a TV that is off when Kodi starts and is turned on; Deep Colour turned on; a
DV TV replaced by one without, and back; QFT turned on (Low-Latency only); a DV TV that wakes up
(no EDID: HDR10 kept, native output waits a video); an EDID that cannot be read; and an
unchanged TV (100 videos: 100 reads of the kernel's EDID, no probe, nothing parsed; a DV TV
probed as since 1.0 RC1, nothing parsed). Seven mutations of the decisions make it fail.
